---
import ResearchLayout from '../../layouts/ResearchLayout.astro';
import ProjectSection from '../../components/ProjectSection.astro';
---

<ResearchLayout
  title="Growth-Oriented Practice in Psychosis"
  description="A doctoral research project exploring how mental health professionals can recognise and support growth following psychosis. Led by Sarah Bowles at Salomons Institute, Canterbury Christ Church University."
  pathname="/research/growth-oriented-practice-in-psychosis"
>

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="border-b border-border">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 md:px-8 pt-16 md:pt-24 pb-20 md:pb-28">
      <p class="font-accent text-2xl md:text-3xl text-poster-teal mb-4">
        A doctoral research project
      </p>
      <h1 class="font-display font-semibold text-4xl md:text-5xl lg:text-6xl tracking-tight text-ink" style="text-decoration-color: var(--color-poster-teal)">
        Growth-Oriented Practice in Psychosis
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 font-body text-lg md:text-xl text-muted leading-relaxed">
        What helps people grow through and beyond experiences of psychosis?
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 1: Why this matters -->
  <ProjectSection id="why-this-matters" title="Why this matters" accent="teal">
    <p>
      Psychosis is frequently traumatic, and the experience can cause significant distress and
      disruption. A growing body of research, however, shows that people often also experience
      meaningful growth in its aftermath. This growth can take many forms: a renewed sense of
      purpose, deeper relationships, a stronger sense of self, new priorities, or a richer
      spiritual or existential life.
    </p>
    <p>
      Despite this body of evidence, mental health services rarely have clear models for how
      clinicians can recognise and actively support this kind of growth alongside clinical
      recovery. Without such guidance, clinicians may inadvertently invalidate people's
      experiences or work with the unrealistic expectation that everyone who experiences
      psychosis should grow.
    </p>
    <p>
      This research aims to begin to fill that gap.
    </p>
  </ProjectSection>

  <!-- Section 2: What this research asks -->
  <ProjectSection id="what-this-research-asks" title="What this research asks" accent="coral">
    <p>
      The study explores four questions, from the perspective of clinicians who actively try
      to promote growth following psychosis:
    </p>
    <ol class="space-y-4 mt-2 list-none">
      <li class="flex gap-4">
        <span class="font-body font-semibold text-poster-coral shrink-0 w-6">1.</span>
        <span>How do they understand the ways in which growth can occur following psychosis?</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-4">
        <span class="font-body font-semibold text-poster-coral shrink-0 w-6">2.</span>
        <span>How does that understanding shape their clinical practice?</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-4">
        <span class="font-body font-semibold text-poster-coral shrink-0 w-6">3.</span>
        <span>What are the core elements of growth-oriented practice, and when should they be applied?</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-4">
        <span class="font-body font-semibold text-poster-coral shrink-0 w-6">4.</span>
        <span>What cultural and ethical considerations shape this kind of work?</span>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </ProjectSection>

  <!-- Section 3: A growing evidence base -->
  <ProjectSection id="evidence-base" title="A growing evidence base" accent="sage">
    <p>
      The idea that psychosis can lead to growth is supported by an expanding research
      literature. A 2018 systematic review synthesised 40 studies of positive change after a
      first episode of psychosis, finding consistent reports of growth at individual,
      interpersonal, and spiritual levels (Jordan et al., 2018). These findings are often
      framed through the lens of post-traumatic growth, the theory that struggling with
      adversity can lead to positive psychological change including greater appreciation for
      life, more meaningful relationships, a sense of personal strength, changed priorities,
      and a stronger spiritual or existential life (Tedeschi &amp; Calhoun, 2004).
    </p>
    <p>
      Other theoretical perspectives have also tried to explain growth following psychosis.
      Jackson's Paradigm Shifting Process Model, for instance, proposes that some psychotic
      experiences may contain insights that allow a person to resolve an underlying internal
      conflict, leading to a meaningful shift in how they understand themselves and the world
      (Jackson, 2010).
    </p>
    <p>
      Despite all this work, very little research has asked clinicians themselves how they
      think about growth following psychosis and how they try to support it. This study is one
      of the first to do so.
    </p>
  </ProjectSection>

</ResearchLayout>
